{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>That his days may be prolonged over his kingdom.</b> From the affirmative you infer the negative. And so we find with Saul: because he transgressed a minor commandment — for the prophet said to him (I Samuel 10:8), ״שבעת ימים תוחל עד באי אליך״ — He said to him (I Samuel 13), ״נסכלת לא שמרת״ and so forth, ״ועתה ממלכתך לא תקום״." "<b>He and his sons.</b> Scripture teaches you that the kingship is an inheritance, and there is no need to anoint a king who is the son of a king — only the first one alone. But if a dispute has fallen among Israel and they do not agree who shall reign, the one whom all agree to enthrone requires anointing. This is what is said: ״בקרב ישראל״ — and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted it: at a time when there is peace upon Israel — that is, when there is peace in Israel, the kingship is an inheritance and does not require anointing. And the fact that they anointed Solomon, though he was the son of a king — our Rabbis of blessed memory already interpreted this: because of the dispute of Adonijah son of Haggith."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:49:04.336215157Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}